---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { derived, massBudget, MTOW_CAP_G } from "../data/ledger";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// No hero photograph, deliberately. The first thing on this page is the
// ledger, rendered from src/data/ledger.ts — the course's actual subject,
// drawn from the file every other page also reads.
const LABELS: Record<keyof typeof massBudget, string> = {
  wingStructure: "Wing structure",
  fuselageStructure: "Fuselage",
  tailStructure: "Tail",
  propulsion: "Motor, ESC, propeller",
  battery: "Battery",
  avionics: "Avionics",
  servos: "Servos and linkages",
  payload: "Payload",
  consumables: "Fasteners and finish",
};

const lines = Object.entries(massBudget).map(([key, grams]) => ({
  label: LABELS[key as keyof typeof massBudget],
  grams,
  percent: (grams / MTOW_CAP_G) * 100,
}));
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    You get {MTOW_CAP_G} grams. Everything the aircraft is made of, everything it
    carries and everything that makes it go has to come out of that number, and
    so does the battery that has to lift all of it. Twelve weeks later you fly
    what the arithmetic left you.
  </p>

  <table class="ledger">
    <caption>The mass budget, at the course design point. Every page on this site reads these numbers from one file.</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Line</th><th scope="col">Grams</th><th scope="col">Share of the cap</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {lines.map((line) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{line.label}</th>
          <td class="num">{line.grams}</td>
          <td><span class="bar" style={`--w:${line.percent.toFixed(1)}%`}></span></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
      <tr class="reserve">
        <th scope="row">Unallocated reserve</th>
        <td class="num">{derived.reserveG}</td>
        <td><span class="bar bar-reserve" style={`--w:${((derived.reserveG / MTOW_CAP_G) * 100).toFixed(1)}%`}></span></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You fly in week 1, on somebody else's aircraft, on a buddy box, before you
    have made a single design decision. Then you spend eleven weeks finding out
    what it costs to build your own: a mission written as numbered requirements,
    a wing sized for a stall speed you chose, a battery picked because the
    cruise power said so, a spar that survives a load case, and a fly-off in
    week 12 where the thing either does what you said it would or does not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every week you predict a number and then go and measure it. The gap between
    those two is most of what this course teaches, and most of what it marks.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Second-year engineering students who would rather be held to a number than
    to an opinion. You need first-year mechanics and enough programming to read
    a flight log. You do not need to have flown anything: week 1 exists for
    exactly that reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a twelve-unit intensive and it is about double an ordinary course.
    Four people, one aircraft, one ledger, all semester.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>Twelve weeks, each with a field objective your team books its own slot to fly.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Six items, adding to a hundred, every group one carrying the same systems-engineering row.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Field days" href="/field-days/">
      <p>How slots are booked at Mount Sludge, who is on duty, and what stops a session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches this, and who at the club keeps you from hurting yourself.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-block-end: 2rem;
  }

  .ledger {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin-block-end: 2.5rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .ledger caption {
    text-align: left;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    padding-block-end: 0.75rem;
  }

  .ledger th,
  .ledger td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.75rem 0.35rem 0;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-border, currentColor);
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .ledger thead th {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .ledger .num {
    text-align: right;
    width: 5rem;
  }

  .ledger td:last-child {
    width: 55%;
  }

  .bar {
    display: block;
    width: var(--w);
    height: 0.75rem;
    background: var(--at-text-primary, currentColor);
  }

  .bar-reserve {
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      45deg,
      currentColor 0 2px,
      transparent 2px 5px
    );
  }

  .reserve th,
  .reserve td {
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .ledger td:last-child {
      display: none;
    }
    .ledger caption {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
    }
  }
</style>
